      "mainEntity": [{
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does custom cabinetry take?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most custom cabinetry projects take 3-6 weeks from design approval to installation. Smaller projects like a single bathroom vanity or entertainment center can be completed in 3-4 weeks, while extensive kitchen cabinet installations or whole-home built-in projects take 5-6 weeks. The timeline includes fabrication time, delivery, and professional installation. We provide a detailed schedule in your proposal."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What's the difference between custom cabinets and semi-custom?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Custom cabinets are built to your exact specifications for your specific space. Every dimension, every shelf height, every finish detail is designed for you. Semi custom cabinets start with standard sizes that can be modified slightly. Custom Chicago cabinets eliminate wasted space, fit perfectly in challenging layouts, accommodate your exact storage needs, and can match architectural details. For Chicago homes with unique dimensions or historic features, custom is often the only option that truly works."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much do custom cabinets cost in Chicago?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Custom cabinets Chicago pricing typically ranges from $2,000 for small bathroom vanities to $20,000+ for complete kitchen cabinet installations with premium materials. The main cost factors are linear footage of cabinetry, wood species and finish quality, hardware selections, complexity of design, and installation requirements. A typical custom kitchen cabinet project in Chicago costs $15,000-$25,000. Custom built-ins for entertainment centers or home offices typically cost $3,000-$10,000. We provide detailed, transparent proposals."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you provide design services for custom cabinetry?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, design consultation is included with every custom cabinetry project. We measure your space precisely, discuss how you'll use the storage, review material and finish options, create detailed plans showing dimensions and layout, and refine the design until it's exactly what you need. Our goal is cabinetry that looks great and functions perfectly for how you actually live."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you match existing cabinetry or woodwork?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, we regularly match existing cabinets, trim, or architectural details in Chicago homes. This is especially important in historic homes or when adding cabinets to kitchens in phases. We can match wood species, stain colors, door styles, and finish details. We bring samples to ensure accurate matching before fabrication begins."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What types of wood do you use for custom cabinets?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We work with a full range of cabinet-grade woods including maple, cherry, oak, walnut, and hickory for traditional options, and painted finishes on maple or MDF for contemporary looks. Each wood has different characteristics for grain, color, and durability. We help you select the best material for your design style, budget, and how the cabinets will be used."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you create built-ins for odd-shaped spaces?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Absolutely. Custom built-ins excel in challenging spaces: alcoves with unusual dimensions, rooms with angled walls, spaces around windows or architectural features, areas with low ceilings or soffits, and layouts where standard furniture simply won't fit. We measure precisely and design solutions that make awkward spaces feel intentional and functional."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do custom cabinets increase home value?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, quality custom cabinetry typically provides strong return on investment, especially in Chicago's competitive real estate market. Custom kitchen cabinets are particularly valuable, often returning 60-80% of investment at resale while you enjoy them daily. Built-in storage solutions also appeal to buyers because they maximize usable space in ways that furniture cannot."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What's your payment schedule for custom cabinetry?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Our standard payment schedule is: (1) Deposit upon contract signing and design approval (typically 40-50%), (2) Payment when cabinets are fabricated and ready for delivery, (3) Final payment upon installation completion and your satisfaction. We never ask for full payment upfront, and we don't request final payment until you're completely satisfied."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you install cabinets in condos with building restrictions?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, we're highly experienced with Chicago condo installations and building requirements. We coordinate elevator reservations, respect noise restrictions and work hours, protect common areas during delivery, submit required documentation to management, and ensure installations meet building codes. We've worked in hundreds of Chicago buildings and understand the unique requirements."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

